{# Who else you could fly, from the same airports.

   Not "similar airlines", which this data cannot tell you, and not a random
   handful either: these are the carriers with the most departures from this
   airline's own hubs, so the list is competition rather than decoration. It
   reads true because hubs carry geography -- Air Canada gets WestJet, Air
   France gets easyJet and Lufthansa, Philippine Airlines gets Cebu Pacific.

   The count is their departures from those same airports, which is what says
   who is actually there: WestJet flies 1,578 out of Air Canada's three hubs
   and Alaska 111, and a list in any other order would present them as equals.

   Same chip the route page uses for its carriers, mark and all. #}
<ul class="chips">
    {% for peer in peers %}
        <li>
            <a class="chip chip--link" href="{{ peer.url }}">
                <img class="chip__logo"
                     src="{{ cdn(config('site.static.endpoint.images') ~ '/suppliers/' ~ peer.code ~ '.png') }}"
                     width="20" height="20" loading="lazy" alt="">
                {{ peer.name }}
                <span class="chip__note">{{ peer.flights|number_format }} flights</span>
            </a>
        </li>
    {% endfor %}
</ul>

<p class="chips__note">Departures from the same airports in the next {{ window_days }} days.</p>
